A||||13||Spektrum-87GT||learning that cars are an expensive hobby||||1||
Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||10-26-2001||11:56 PM||||*warning, this is a 'me' post  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">*<BR>well, totalled up how much everything is going to cost me... for everything I want to do... the total cost, including the price i paid for my fiero is 10k. please give me some support on this! being a practical person(and the fact i only make 20k/year), i say this is too much for a weekend car. The other part of me tells me that these cars are my life(they basically are besides my job).<P>Here's a break down:<BR>87 fiero gt     $1200<BR>v8 engine swap  $4000<BR>custom exhaust  $120  (got a welder)<BR>brake upgrade   $750<BR>paint job       $1500<BR>fastback scoops $120<BR>trunk scoop     $80<BR>steering wheel  $160<BR>shifter w/knob  $50  (got a welder  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<BR>suspension      $800<BR>tires/wheels    $1200<BR>various stuff  ~$1000 (for low estimates and other crap that pops up)<P>if it seems i've missed something, like an alignment or clutch for example. They're added in to what they're related to.<P>so what does everyone think? is it worth it? good plan? it just seems like i'm going to sink so much money into this car!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||10-27-2001||12:18 AM||||Just my opinion, but you don't make enough money a year right now to support your dream.<BR>Why don't you just keep the car stock and enjoy it?! Use some of your surplus cash to bring it back to showroom condition.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BlackStang95||10-27-2001||12:38 AM||||We've already enjoyed the car stock.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> and its not worth spending that kinda money to restore to stock.  He forgets he lives at home and has basically no bills  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||10-27-2001||12:42 AM||||I could save him a lot of money........just send me the car.......see how much you will save??<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektrum-87GT||10-27-2001||12:43 AM||||yeah, forgot to mention i live at home, so no bills  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||10-27-2001||12:52 AM||||Add at least 5k for stuff that you don't know you need to replace.<BR>Or just budget 25k to completely redo the car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||10-27-2001||02:50 AM||||25k?  Dang!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Don't buy a $160 steering wheel.  You can pick up a nice Grant GT wheel for about $60.  Save the money for the scoops so you can have everything you need when you're ready for paint.  Or just forget the steering wheel for now and start saving money for more important things.<P>Does it run?  If it does, I'd put the V8 swap down on the list a few spots.  Go in stages, get the outside looking good and painted one season...  plan and save up over a winter and then have rims/tires ready for spring...  then plan and save up for suspension stuff... then save again and do the brake stuff... save again and then stuff in the big V8 stuff.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>See where I'm getting at?  If you try to do everything at one time you'll go broke in a hurry and then won't be able to even drive it with it all apart. <P>With a budget it takes time, I know. *sigh*<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue4.gif">    <BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR><A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/tatooine/spielberg/241/" TARGET=_blank>Tigger's Fiero Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Nashco||10-27-2001||03:47 AM||||WHOA...you guys have it all wrong! Ok, here's my thinking:<P>First of all, a lot of those prices could be cut in half if you are tight on cash.  If you spend extra time doing research, finding good deals on parts, doing your own work, finding a friend who can help, etc...  Some of us are just at a point in time where we have more time than money, which allows this.<P>Secondly, I don't think that it takes a specific amount of cash to rebuild/upgrade your car.  Yes, in the long run, you will have spent a lot of money; if you only do one thing at a time though, over a weekend or two and as you can afford it, the costs don't hit so hard.  Many of us don't have a second car, so it's not possible to do everything at once anyway.  I am constantly doing something little to my car, which seems pointless one thing at a time, but after a while things really start to add up.  I hadn't realized all I'd done to my car until actually telling somebody, and I had about 20 things I'd done!  Baby steps...take baby steps.<P>Third, I think 5k is a little much of an overshoot on "extra" expenses.  Most of my unforeseen costs so far have been of inconsequential amounts, and are of minor costs.  If you find something that costs a lot and you hadn't expected it, perhaps it wasn't really that important <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Fourth, save paint for last!  If you're doing all this work over a longer period of time, wait to make it pretty after it's a good solid machine.  Make it reliable and driveable first.  After this, I think you should upgrade in the order of suspension, brakes, tires/wheels, scoops, engine, paint.  This way you have a good driver, something with enough brakes to stop a v8 and overpower the tires, tires to compensate for better brakes (with wheels for oversized tires), scoops to make sure you have adequate cooling for the v8 and get body work all prepped, engine swap last after all the rest of the drivetrain is ready for it (figure your 1000 extra will go here mostly), and the paint once it's all complete.  No point in getting some attraction until you really want to show it off <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Which is more fun, showing your work in progress, or showing the progress from your work(finished car, if you believe that's possible)?<P>Ok, those are all just my opinion...some like a pretty car more than solid suspension, but I am not that person at all!  I don't care what it looks like if it will get me to and fro for a while.<P>Good luck!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||AusFiero||10-27-2001||05:52 AM||||I feel your pain Spektrum. I had over $32,000 tied up in my Fieo before the garage caught fire.<BR>But from experince nice cars take money. Have had 47 cars to date so I consider myself sort of an expert on spending money on cars. If that is agood thing? I do not know.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero2.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000009||Screwie||10-27-2001||07:32 AM||||Well Ausfiero, if you had fun doing it, I don't think it's a bad thing.<P>And Spektrum, sounds like you're gonna get yourself one sweeeeeet Fiero there  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>When spending money, I always look at the alternatives, what would I have done with it otherwise, and will that be as much fun. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||10-27-2001||02:44 PM||||cars are cheap, try flying or boating(ie costs me over $100 @ hr to fly the plane I own, and its paid for)||||reg||1
Z||000011||MinnGreenGT||10-27-2001||02:59 PM||||Yeah, cars aren't cheap... especailly if you are planning upgrades. I've had my GT for 6 years now... it's only been drivable for about 2 seasons now.  Prior to that I hadn't driven it for more than 2-3 weeks at a time before another major project HAD to be done (paint body origionally- didn't plan for the others).  Paint, then Engine (3.1 took longer than normal rebuild), then tranny (4spd OD tranny also took longer), then exhaust and other misc.  Now I'm looking to other long-term upgrades- V8 in the GT, Caddy V8 in my old beater SE, new paint in the next few years (with new hood and deck scoops, and maybe Stage-2 from AUS).  It all takes time, and it all takes money- like others have stated... take your time and spread it out!  Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Spektrum-87GT||10-27-2001||10:00 PM||||thanks for all the advice, you have no idea how much its helped me decide. I have another car that I use as my daily driver, so I really dont care how long the car is off the road, it would be awesome to have it all done before I put it back on the road. A big raise is coming, along with the opportunity for a lot of overtime, not to mention a second job, so money is no longer an issue. here's my plan, in 'stages'  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>stage 1: brake upgrade<BR>stage 2: suspension<BR>stage 3: v8 engine swap<BR>stage 4: custom exhaust<BR>stage 5: tires/wheels<BR>stage 6: scoops<BR>stage 7: paint<BR>stage 8: interior<P>plans might change after stage 3, I'm working a deal out with the guy at <A HREF="http://www.exoticrebodies.com" TARGET=_blank>www.exoticrebodies.com</A>  for a diabolic roadster kit, wont know until next spring. I'm also thinking it would be a good idea to have a lot of money behind me before I start in on this. thanks again for all the help!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fformula88||10-27-2001||11:39 PM||||Since you have a daily driver, there is no need for you to get this project done tomorrow.  Take things slow as you can afford them and do one project at a time.  In the meantime, if its driveable, go ahead and take it out.<P>I bought my Fiero in 1997 with goals like yours!  At the time I was a sophmore in college with sky high dreams and no cash flow  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  So far I have only been able to address cosmetic issues: it needed a new rear wing and right quarter window, engraved intake plenum, headliner, plus I added a leather steering wheel, Fiero emblem embroidered floor mats and a few other small details.  <P>Its looking great now, and I now have the $$ for other mods.  Since my rear springs are sagging I plan on attacking the suspension first.  New sport shocks/struts and lowering springs are going all around.  I also want to get all new poly bushings!<P>From there I am going to do a V6 conversion.  I want something a little easier than a V8 and am planning on a GM 3.4L crate engine.  I'd like to do performance heads, injectors, and a few other small items!  I don't need a road rocket, but do want some more horses!  Even this may not happen for a few years, but when I am done with it, it will have been worth it.<P>Just stay patient.  Do what you can when you can.  That way, your sure to get what you want and not compromise in a rush job!||||reg||1
